    3	The first thing Cael saw from the gallery was a red book.
    4	
    5	He had come up the east hall's gallery stair on the second morning before the first bell, because whatever Rooke had built in here over the recess he had built for one of the four, and Cael wanted its shape in his notebook while it was still new enough to show its joints. He took the rail at the north end, with the high windows at his back, and looked down. Below him, at a trestle under a lamp it did not need, a clerk sat over a book bound in red board, turning a page with one wet finger.
    6	
    7	Then he saw what the book was for. Rooke had stripped the room. The boards were planed back to bare wood, and across them, in fresh white paint, ran no rings at all, only groups of short strokes and small crosses and arrows, as if somebody had written a dance down for people he did not trust to dance it. Even the striking frames along the south wall were strangers. The padded posts stood on springs as thick as a man's wrist, rigged to throw back a Silver's blow instead of swallowing a Copper's.
    8	
    9	Five people were working on the floor.
   10	
   11	Rooke stood by the trestle with his board under his arm and his pencil behind his ear. At the far end two third-years went up and down a line of crosses: a broad-backed Force, gone up to Rank Seven at the year's last sitting, who moved as if he expected the floor to argue with him, and a lean Current girl who never seemed to put her heels down at all. The fourth was a stranger, and Cael knew him at once. The fifth was Lira.
   12	
   13	The stranger was a Stone, thick through the shoulders and slow in the feet, and he stood at the near end of the painted lines with his hands folded in front of him like a man waiting outside a shop. Cael had seen him last at the wool town, on the far side of a rope, losing to Lira. He had been an Iron of the river academy then, its first entry, and she had beaten him by finding the half a beat in which neither of his planted feet was reading the floor, and going through it. Now he was a Silver, newly advanced, and he had sewn nothing on his collar to say so. Over the recess, Lira had said, he had written to Rooke and asked for the posting, and brought his academy's drill-book with him in a red board cover so that the bluff could copy it.
