    3	On the fourth evening the rota found him at supper.
    4	
    5	The refectory ran the length of the old range under the bell tower, three long tables and a short one under the windows, and the house had always sat where it liked. Cael sat where he always sat, at the end of the middle table by the pillar, with his back to the wall and the door in front of him. Lira was across from him, eating as if somebody might take the plate away. Brom was beside her with two plates. Karis had a book propped against the salt.
    6	
    7	Seln sat between Brom and Karis, at the middle of the bench.
    8	
    9	He had sat there every evening since the first waystation on the road home. Nobody had said a word about it then and nobody said one now. He ate slowly, with his elbows in, and he cut his bread into exact squares before he buttered any of it, and when Brom pushed the dish of beans along to him he took some and pushed it on to Karis without looking up. It was the most ordinary thing in the room. Cael counted it anyway, every evening, the way a man counts heads at a door at the end of a long day.
   10	
   11	At the short table under the windows, two second-years were watching him eat.
   12	
   13	They were not good at it. One of them was a Mire boy who kept glancing up and then down again as if the ceiling had spoken to him. The other was a girl with ink on her collar, and she was much better. She did not glance at all. She ate her supper, and turned the pages of a small notebook beside her plate with one finger, and every so often she wrote a word in it without lifting her eyes from Cael's hands.
   14	
   15	"Your assistant," he said to Karis.
   16	
   17	"Yes." Karis did not look up from her book. "She asked me last week how a person learns to observe. I told her to choose a subject who would never notice he was being watched, and to keep a page on him for a month." She turned a leaf. "She chose you. I've entered it as tomorrow's error."
   18	
