     1	# Chapter 5 — Served
     2	
     3	The eighth day of the year was a First-day, hot and still, and at the second bell Cael was on the covered walk with a book under his arm and nowhere in particular to be.
     4	
     5	The house had found its feet by then. A week was long enough for that. The intake had stopped walking in groups of six and started walking in groups of two. The boards along the walk had filled up with the year's ordinary paper: the research standings, the program's floor hours, the refectory's rota, and a sheet in Ephram's square hand announcing that the captain would hear grievances on Third-day evenings in the common room and would not be held responsible for his answers. The fire-watch boy's slate hung on its nail under the north arch with last night's hours chalked down it in large careful figures, the last of them rubbed out once and made again.
     6	
     7	Cael stopped at the slate, because he always did, and then he looked east, because the arch looked east, and saw them.
     8	
     9	Three riders, on the last switchback below the gate.
    10	
    11	They were coming up at a walk. Two rode a little ahead and to either side, in grey with a badge on the shoulder, and they rode the way men ride who have been told the pace and are keeping it whether it suits the horses or not. The third rode between them. He sat very upright, and across the front of his saddle, strapped tight against the horse's withers so that it could not shift, lay a case of dark leather the length of his forearm.
    12	
    13	Couriers trotted. Every courier Cael had ever watched on that road had come up it at a working trot with his eyes on the next bend, because a courier was paid by the day and the road was long. These three walked, and kept step, the way men walk behind something they have been told not to hurry.
    14	
    15	He looked up at the records hall. The high window at its north end was empty, and the ship's glass was gone from it. Somewhere inside, a man who had watched that road for eight days had already put the glass in his coat and gone down to his counter.
    16	
    17	At the gate the lodge-man came out of his door and stood as straight as he stood for the Chancellor's carriage, and for nothing else. Riders on registry business did not stop for lodge-men. They passed under the arch at the same walk, still in the saddle, and the lodge-man stepped back against the wall with his hat in his hand and let them. They crossed the outer court with their hooves loud on the stones in the heat, and every head on the covered walk turned to follow them, and they went on round the corner of the lecture range toward the records hall as if nobody on the hill had a right to stop them, which was true.
    18	
    19	Cael put his book under his other arm and went after them.
    20	
    21	He did not run. A boy running across a court after a registry escort would have been a story by noon. He walked fast, at the pace of somebody late for a lecture, and he came round the corner of the lecture range in time to see the third rider dismount at the records-hall steps and unstrap the case from his saddle. The two outriders stayed mounted. They sat their horses at the foot of the steps, one on each side, facing outward.
    22	
    23	Cael went past them and up the steps and in. Nobody stopped him. It would not have occurred to him to wait outside.
    24	
    25	The records hall was cool after the court, and dim, and very quiet.
    26	
