    28	
    29	The rider came in behind Cael with the case under his arm.
    30	
    31	On his feet he was a different kind of man from the two outside. He had soft hands and good boots that had not been walked in, and a clerk's grey coat kept very clean, with a small seal of office in dull silver at the collar that Cael did not know. He was perhaps fifty, with thin hair combed close and the careful face of somebody who has set a great many things down on a great many counters and has never once been set down himself. He set the case on the counter and laid his hand on it and waited.
    32	
    33	Withrow came in from the inner door thirty seconds later.
    34	
    35	She came down the long hall as if she had been sent for about a gutter. Cael had watched her cross courts and corridors for two years and knew the walk as well as he knew Brom's step on a stair, even and unhurried, the walk of a woman who had decided long ago that nothing would ever be allowed to set its pace. With half the hill already talking outside, he understood that she had chosen it on the stair and was holding it now the way a carter holds a nervous horse.
    36	
    37	She stopped at the counter beside Bracken, and the officer bowed to her, to a depth that somebody had once measured, and took a card from his coat.
    38	
    39	"An instrument of the registry, under seal," he read from the card, in a voice with nothing in it. "For joint service upon the Chancellor of Halcenvane Academy and upon the enrollee of record whom it names, the two together and in their own persons. Service to be acknowledged in both their hands. The seal to stay whole until it is." He lowered the card. "Are both parties present?"
    40	
    41	"Both parties are present," said Withrow.
    42	
    43	She did not look at Cael. She did not need to. The officer looked at him, once, the way a man checks a figure against the sum he was given, and looked away again.
    44	
