    90	
    91	He took the library stair two at a time, all four flights, with the hip reminding him at every landing that it had been promised a quieter week.
    92	
    93	The door at the top was shut. The card was in its brass holder. The new lock was locked.
    94	
    95	He knocked, and the key turned on the inside, and Karis opened the door with a pen in her hand and ink on the side of her thumb and the look of somebody who has been interrupted in the middle of a sentence and means to go back to it.
    96	
    97	Then she saw his face, and the look went.
    98	
    99	"What," she said.
   100	
   101	"A registry instrument. Under escort. Served jointly on Withrow and me in the records hall ten minutes ago." He made himself say it in order, the way she would want it. "A challenge to the enrollment basis. Fourteen days to answer. Withrow read the first page aloud. Bracken is making you a copy now."
   102	
   103	Karis stood in the doorway and did not move.
   104	
   105	He told her the first page. He had it nearly whole; he had been listening the way he listened to anything that would have to be written down later. *Does not allege fraud. Nor contest the text.* *Evaluation integrity.* *Compelled re-evaluation under standard Arbiter protocol.* *Stand or fall.* She listened to all of it without interrupting, with her pen still in her hand, and when he had finished she looked past him, down the narrow stair, for a long moment.
   106	
   107	"Standard Arbiter protocol," she said. "Those words? Exactly those?"
   108	
   109	"Exactly those."
   110	
   111	She was quiet. He knew what she was seeing, because he was seeing it too. Standard protocol meant a station: a frame in a stone room, a registry officer at its side, a practitioner standing in it while the frame read him. It was how every Path on the continent had been declared since before the registry kept books. The frame read the Path and named it, and set a tier against it, and a rank inside the tier, and the officer wrote down what the frame had said, and that was a person's classification for life. It was how Lira had been declared, and Brom, and Karis herself. It was how the frame at Denvash had been asked, on the morning of his Kindling, what he was, and had answered with one word in brackets and nothing else at all.
   112	
   113	The demonstration provision had never asked a frame anything. That was the whole of why it held him.
