     1	# Chapter 6 — The Records Hall
     2	
     3	The fire-watch kept an oil book. Every lamp lit on the hill after the tenth bell went into it, by building and by hour, so that the bursar could see at the year's end where the house had burned its money in the dark. Bracken signed it himself every morning, and had done for thirteen years, and in all that time the records hall had never once had a line in it.
     4	
     5	At a little past the eighth bell he took the book down from its hook by the side door, opened it on the counter at the night's page, and wrote in his small upright hand.
     6	
     7	*Records hall. Lamps late. On the registrar's authority, and at his charge.*
     8	
     9	Then he hung it back on its hook and went to light the lamps.
    10	
