    36	
    37	It was a list. Ninety-one lines, in Karis's smallest finding hand, one invocation to a line, each with its year and its district and the section called on and a few words of what it had been about. She had set them out district by district, as the digests kept them, not by year. She put her finger beside line sixty-three, and took it away.
    38	
    39	*Ostrand district station; sigil frame reading Stone practitioners one rank above their true rank over one month; integrity provisions, section four; filed by the registrar of Halcenvane in his fourth year in office; frame withdrawn, tested a fortnight, a seam in the casing found admitting damp, repaired, the month's Stones re-read; sustained.*
    40	
    41	Bracken looked at it for a long time.
    42	
    43	Then he put on his spectacles and looked at it again, more closely, and Cael watched the registrar read his own name in a list of ninety-one, in another person's hand, in a document that was going down the hill to answer a petition about a boy who stood two feet away from him on the other side of the counter.
    44	
    45	"I'd forgotten I wrote it that way round," said Bracken at last. "*Admitting damp.* I'd have said *letting in* now." He was still looking at the line. "In my fourth year I thought a registrar ought to sound like a registrar."
    46	
    47	"The original's in the registry's file," said Karis. "I copied the citation from the digest. There's one thing." She took the leaf back and turned it over, to a note at its foot. "In your filing, you spelt *casing* with two esses. I corrected it in the citation. Silently. It's a convention."
    48	
    49	Bracken took his spectacles off.
    50	
    51	"Don't," he said.
